WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.680
Fan outrage didn't just start in the Twitter era.&nbsp;
Get ready to learn about the "X-Files" episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:11.760
that was too gross, the "Seinfeld" finale outcry,&nbsp;
and more '90s TV moments that had fans fuming.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:18.880
The X-Files is no stranger to unnatural horrors.&nbsp;
Throughout most of the 90s, it shocked, awed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:24.000
delighted, and disgusted its devoted fanbase&nbsp;
with stories featuring supernatural terrors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.680
extraterrestrial threats, and&nbsp;
other disturbing subject matter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:33.280
And perhaps no other episode generated&nbsp;
quite as much controversy as 1996's "Home,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:37.600
part of the show's fourth season. The&nbsp;
horrifying events in this particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:43.360
episode stemmed not from alien appendages&nbsp;
or paranormal palms, but human hands.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:47.680
"Home" kicks off with protagonists Fox&nbsp;
Mulder and Dana Scully investigating the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:52.560
circumstances behind the death of a deformed&nbsp;
baby. This leads them to the Peacocks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:58.000
a family with some truly gruesome secrets. It&nbsp;
turns out that they're actually three inbred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.240
brothers who are hiding the existence&nbsp;
of their quadruple amputee mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.480
who had been breeding with them over&nbsp;
the years. By the end of the episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:11.440
she and one of her children successfully&nbsp;
escape, ready to grow their family once again.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:16.640
The sheer gruesomeness of "Home" earned&nbsp;
it a TV-MA rating upon its debut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:21.200
a first in the history of American TV&nbsp;
programming. Fans and network executives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:25.840
found the episode so revolting that Fox&nbsp;
took it out of circulation for three years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:30.160
The episode isn't without its defenders,&nbsp;
though. The A.V. Club, for one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:36.000
included it on a list of "10 must-see&nbsp;
episodes of The X-Files" in a 2012 article.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.120
"You know your boy will do&nbsp;
anything for his mother."

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:46.480
For most of its run, the ABC sitcom Roseanne&nbsp;
enjoyed fairly stable popularity and reviews.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:51.120
It featured the blue-collar life of Roseanne&nbsp;
Conner and her family, and it resonated with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.600
audiences thanks to its relatability and&nbsp;
surprisingly progressive ways of tackling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:01.680
real-world themes. And then Season 9 aired, and&nbsp;
everything went completely off the rails. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:08.080
was the final season of the show's original run&nbsp;
before it was rebooted in 2018, and the big change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:13.680
was due to the Conners winning the lottery.
 
"We won the lottery! This is the winning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:17.200
lottery ticket! I've got it right here!&nbsp;
Remember you told me to watch it on TV?"

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:19.680
"What are you talking about?"

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:25.280
From ordinary working-class struggles, the&nbsp;
show's focus shifted to pop culture parody and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:30.960
self-conscious approach. Additionally, Roseanne's&nbsp;
husband Dan was sidelined for most of the season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:35.840
explained away by subplots involving him taking&nbsp;
care of his mother and cheating on his wife.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:41.280
The finale didn't offer much satisfaction.&nbsp;
It completely invalidated the entire season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.480
by revealing that it was all happening in&nbsp;
Roseanne's imagination. Dan had actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:50.560
died from a heart attack after Season 8,&nbsp;
the Conners didn't really win the lottery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:54.720
and Roseanne was actually writing her&nbsp;
memoir as she spiraled further down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.720 --> 00:03:00.000
into sadness. It was the show's lowest-rated&nbsp;
season, and it left a bad taste in fans'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.920
mouths that wasn't fully washed away until the&nbsp;
revival undid some of the most hated decisions.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:11.680
Lasting nine seasons from 1989 to 1998,&nbsp;
Seinfeld was one of the most successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:16.640
and beloved American sitcoms of the decade.&nbsp;
Featuring comedian Jerry Seinfeld delivering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:21.520
his signature brand of observational humor, the&nbsp;
so-called "show about nothing" featured him and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:26.400
his three friends George, Elaine, and Kramer in&nbsp;
mundane situations that brought out the best,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:31.520
or worst, in them in New York City. One&nbsp;
infamous example was the 1997 episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:36.240
"The Puerto Rican Day," which invited a&nbsp;
ton of backlash to the show's doorstep.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:39.280
The premise was simple enough: the&nbsp;
main characters found themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:43.040
unable to move through traffic because&nbsp;
of the city's Puerto Rican Day parade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:46.800
This triggered a series of events that&nbsp;
led to Kramer accidentally burning the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:52.400
Puerto Rican flag with a sparkler. The outrage in&nbsp;
response to that moment was swift and tremendous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:57.920
as it offended many New Yorkers, particularly the&nbsp;
Hispanic population. This was enough for Columbia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:01.640
TriStar to knock it out of circulation&nbsp;
in New York for almost five years.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:07.040
Among all the reasons why fans would feel&nbsp;
angry and betrayed by their favorite TV show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:09.840
you would think that the lead&nbsp;
character getting a haircut would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:13.920
pretty low on the list. But that's not&nbsp;
how things turned out for Keri Russell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:17.920
star of The WB's college-set drama&nbsp;
Felicity. In one episode that aired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:23.280
in 1999, she cut off her famous curly hair,&nbsp;
which seemingly summoned the apocalypse.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:28.800
In a 2017 interview with W Magazine, Russell&nbsp;
noted that the haircut was intended to be part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:33.440
of the character's post-breakup actions in the&nbsp;
series. And while the actress herself enjoyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:38.800
her new hairstyle, she quickly realized that the&nbsp;
fans didn't share that opinion. As she put it,

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:42.960
"I did not expect all the hysteria.&nbsp;
Strangers did come up to me on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:46.080
street and say things like, 'You were&nbsp;
so pretty before you cut your hair.'"

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:51.200
The show's ratings reportedly plummeted to the&nbsp;
point that WB Entertainment president Susanne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:56.400
Daniels believed that the haircut turned off&nbsp;
many of the show's male fans. As she put it,

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:00.160
"You diluted that icon. I think it&nbsp;
turned some [of the] audience away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.640
in particular men and some women."

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:06.800
The move generated so much negative&nbsp;
buzz that it's even been immortalized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:09.840
in contemporary lexicon as "pulling a Felicity."

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:12.880
"Gutsy move. It's like a whole new you."

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:18.480
If you take a look at Rotten Tomatoes' scoreboard&nbsp;
for each year of Buffy the Vampire Slayer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:23.680
you'll notice that Season 6 sits at the&nbsp;
bottom with 63 percent. This isn't just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:28.720
a case of critics being overly critical. As&nbsp;
recounted by Vanity Fair, the sixth season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:34.480
of the iconic supernatural 90s show was also the&nbsp;
"most hated" by its fans, for numerous reasons.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:38.480
For an important bit of perspective, Season&nbsp;
5 ended with Sarah Michelle Gellar's title&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:43.840
character heroically sacrificing herself to&nbsp;
save the world. Season 6 then dealt with her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:48.080
friends attempting to bring her back and&nbsp;
eventually succeeding. However, the series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:53.920
took an overall depressing tone, mired with weird&nbsp;
plot twists, gratuitous sex, and a protagonist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:58.400
who had trouble dealing with her resurrection.&nbsp;
To make matters worse, the cast reportedly had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:03.440
difficulty communicating their issues with the&nbsp;
story to showrunner Joss Whedon, who was busy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:08.880
balancing his workload with two other shows in&nbsp;
addition to Buffy. Fans even coined a pair of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:14.680
derogatory nicknames for the less-than-stellar&nbsp;
season: "Season Sex" and "Season Sucks."

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:21.200
Debuting in 1994, the ABC sitcom Ellen featured&nbsp;
the day-to-day life of a thirty-something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:26.000
bookstore owner as she balanced the little&nbsp;
struggles and triumphs of life in Los Angeles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:31.760
It catapulted its star, future talk show host&nbsp;
Ellen DeGeneres, to superstardom. This was for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:35.920
two main reasons. For one, it was the vehicle&nbsp;
for many viewers to get acquainted with her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:41.360
quirky sense of humor. And furthermore, the show&nbsp;
made history by having its main character reveal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:46.720
her true sexual orientation at around the same&nbsp;
time the actor playing her did so in real life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:51.200
Unsurprisingly, this move prompted&nbsp;
both congratulations and controversy.

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:56.800
The plan to have the onscreen Ellen come out&nbsp;
on the show had been in the works since 1996.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:02.400
And so, in 1997's two-part "The Puppy Episode,"&nbsp;
she admits the truth to her love interest Susan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:06.640
played by Laura Dern, via an unintentional&nbsp;
and humorous public declaration.

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:12.600
"Susan, I'm gay."

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:21.680
"The Puppy Episode" garnered 42 million viewers,&nbsp;
becoming the highest-rated outing of the series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:27.200
DeGeneres' bold move earned the episode and the&nbsp;
show many accolades, awards, and recognition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:31.760
Unfortunately, it also drew the ire of&nbsp;
multiple advertisers and religious groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:37.200
as well as angry fans who reportedly sent hate&nbsp;
mail and even threatened to bomb the production.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:41.920
Both DeGeneres and Dern suffered career&nbsp;
setbacks after the episode aired, although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:45.520
they've since managed to recover and&nbsp;
continue to work on popular projects.

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:50.480
Sometimes a TV show has a premise so&nbsp;
fundamentally problematic you wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:55.200
how it ever made it onto the air in the first&nbsp;
place. Consider the case of Heil Honey I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:59.840
Home!, a British sitcom that followed a&nbsp;
fictionalized Adolf Hitler and Eva Braun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:04.640
as a quirky couple in a corny 1950s-inspired&nbsp;
setting. Their next-door neighbors were a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:09.760
Jewish couple called the Goldensteins. Only one&nbsp;
episode aired in 1990 before a combination of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:14.320
behind-the-scenes disagreements, production&nbsp;
woes, and protests from groups offended by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:17.760
the idea of trivializing Hitler&nbsp;
took it off the air completely.

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:20.640
In a 2017 interview with Entertainment Weekly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:24.560
series creator Geoff Atkinson explained&nbsp;
the thought process behind the show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:28.960
The idea was that he wanted to beat the&nbsp;
bully by laughing at him. As he put it,

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:31.920
"If you have a monster like that,&nbsp;
and everyone says, 'You can't make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:36.400
fun of him,' then we've made him even more&nbsp;
[of] a monster. That's what fascists want,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:41.080
to keep people in fear of them, when surely&nbsp;
we should be debunking and destroying them."

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:47.760
In December 1997, hundreds of children in Japan&nbsp;
tuned in to get their regular dose of Pokémon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:51.680
But by the end of this particular episode,&nbsp;
they found themselves experiencing seizures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:55.120
and in the days that followed,&nbsp;
many more were rushed to hospitals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.120 --> 00:09:00.000
The root of this medical emergency was a&nbsp;
six-second sequence of bright, flashing lights.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:04.800
The episode, entitled "Electric Soldier Porygon"&nbsp;
generated so much controversy that the show aired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:10.000
no new episodes for four months. The series was&nbsp;
ultimately saved from the jaws of cancellation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:13.200
but Nintendo ended up banning&nbsp;
the episode in all countries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:16.560
Furthermore, the Pokémon character&nbsp;
Porygon and its evolutions have yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:19.920
to appear or display their powers&nbsp;
on the show since the incident.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:25.200
And that's not the end of the story. A 2001 study&nbsp;
looked into the incident and found that the cases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:30.400
of children suffering seizures after the episode&nbsp;
aired may not have been due to epilepsy. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:34.160
they were likely the result of mass&nbsp;
hysteria, a phenomenon in which people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:38.240
experience severe physical symptoms&nbsp;
due to stress or a perceived threat.

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:43.600
Sometimes, even the most well-intentioned&nbsp;
attempts at social awareness can backfire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:47.520
especially when you attempt them in a&nbsp;
children's cartoon. One major example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:52.480
is the 1991 Tiny Toon Adventures episode&nbsp;
"Elephant Issues," whose satirical segment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:56.960
about the dangers of drunk driving caused&nbsp;
it to be banned from reruns on FOX Kids.

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:02.320
The episode's third short, entitled "One Beer,"&nbsp;
features Buster Bunny, Plucky Duck, and Hamton J.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:07.520
Pig engaging in some car-stealing shenanigans&nbsp;
after drinking a single bottle of beer. As soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:12.240
as they get intoxicated, they manage to carjack&nbsp;
a police vehicle. They then end up driving off a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:18.160
cliff, seemingly killing themselves and ascending&nbsp;
into heaven as angels. But then the true punchline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:22.880
arrives seconds later, when the camera pans to&nbsp;
reveal the three characters alive and unscathed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:28.560
in a studio. The whole thing was just a scripted&nbsp;
gag to teach kids about the horrors of alcohol.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:32.960
Despite its important message and clear&nbsp;
self-awareness, it was taken off the air and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:38.560
rarely re-broadcast, reportedly due to backlash&nbsp;
from parents. However, it did eventually make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:44.320
onto the third volume of the Tiny Toon Adventures&nbsp;
DVD collection that was released in 2013.

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:49.840
Nobody really has high expectations concerning&nbsp;
morality and ethics when it comes to Beavis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:54.880
and Butt-Head. The MTV cartoon influenced an&nbsp;
entire generation with its crass and irreverent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.880 --> 00:11:00.320
brand of humor. It's no surprise that a number of&nbsp;
episodes have been edited or even outright banned.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:05.680
But there's one particularly infamous episode&nbsp;
that was completely removed from circulation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:09.120
due to a real-world tragedy it allegedly inspired.

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:15.120
As reported by The New York Times, a five-year-old&nbsp;
boy from Ohio who saw the 1993 episode "Comedians"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:20.080
set his family's mobile home on fire a month&nbsp;
later, leading to the death of his younger sister.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:24.720
The episode features the title characters burning&nbsp;
down a comedy club, and the boy's mother claimed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:29.680
that the episode promoted the idea that playing&nbsp;
with fire was fun. When the local NBC station&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:34.640
reported on the story, they asked some viewers&nbsp;
of the show who they thought bore responsibility.

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:38.400
"I don't think it's MTV's fault at all. I&nbsp;
think it's the parent's fault for maybe letting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:40.560
kids like 5 years old watching the show."

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:45.680
Notably, this wasn't the first time that the show&nbsp;
was associated with a real-world fire in Ohio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:50.400
as there was an incident months earlier in which&nbsp;
three young girls nearly burned their house down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:55.120
According to the police, they admitted that a&nbsp;
Beavis and Butt-Head gag inspired them to use an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:57.920
aerosol can and a match to set clothing on fire.

